.TH WAVE-BASEDIR 1 "" "MacWave" "MacWave Manual"

.SH NAME
wave-basedir \- Print MacWave's install root -- the base_dir in config.json, which is where the binaries, the links and the dependencies live. A path under your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute path as-is and nothing else. With --set it copies the whole tree to a new location and rewrites base_dir; that needs three typed confirmations, and every installed artifact must already be position-independent (run wave transfer --all first).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wave basedir [\-\-raw] | wave basedir \-\-set <path>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print MacWave's install root -- the base_dir in config.json, which is
where the binaries, the links and the dependencies live. A path under
your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute
path as-is and nothing else. With --set it copies the whole tree to a
new location and rewrites base_dir; that needs three typed
confirmations, and every installed artifact must already be
position-independent (run wave transfer --all first).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the absolute path as-is, with no emoji or colour (for scripts)
.TP
.B \-\-set <path>
Move the whole install tree to <path> and update base_dir
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
With --set: report what would be copied and which config file would
change, then stop

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wave basedir
wave basedir --raw
cd "$(wave basedir --raw)"
wave basedir --set ~/apps/macwave
wave basedir --set ~/apps/macwave --dry-run
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
wave(1)
